          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 7"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.085,
            0.884,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " your surroundings and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "notice",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " what animals eat. Animals eat different types of food. Bees and sunbirds suck the nectar of flowers, while infants of humans and many other animals feed on their mother’s milk. Snakes, like python, swallow the animals they prey upon. Some aquatic animals filter tiny food particles floating nearby and feed upon them."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.052,
            0.203,
            0.46,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.052,
                0.203,
                0.46,
                0.556
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_1_image_2.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.166,
                0.563,
                0.432,
                0.581
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 9.1: Human digestive system"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.464,
            0.197,
            0.884,
            0.364
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Animals, including humans, obtain energy from food, which enables them to carry out various life processes. Animals consume food that contains complex components, such as carbohydrate, protein, and fat. These complex food components have to be broken down into simpler forms before the body can use them. But how does this process happen?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.465,
            0.365,
            0.885,
            0.57
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Breaking down of complex food components into simpler forms occurs in a long tube called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "alimentary canal",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". This process starts in the mouth and ends at the anus (Fig. 9.1). As food moves through this canal, digestive juices secreted at different parts break it down into simpler forms. This simpler form of food is absorbed by different parts of our alimentary canal and transported to various parts of our body to carry out various functions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.271,
            0.593,
            0.632,
            0.618
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.1 Nutrition in Animals"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.64,
            0.884,
            0.716
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How do the complex food components get broken down into simpler forms and used by the body in various animals? Is this process the same in all animals or does it vary? Let us first try to understand this process in humans."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.727,
            0.678,
            0.75
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.1.1 Digestion in human beings"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.756,
            0.883,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us trace the journey of food inside our body as it passes through different parts of the alimentary canal."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.802,
            0.587,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Beginning with the mouth cavity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.825,
            0.885,
            0.92
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The journey of the food you eat begins when it enters your mouth. Your teeth break down food you eat into smaller pieces by the processes of crushing and chewing. This process of initial breakdown of food into fine pieces is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "mechanical digestion",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Think about your favourite food. Does your mouth feel watery?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.005,
            0.92,
            0.269,
            0.984
          ],
